# Working in a Minsky session

Minsky runs each task in its own **session workspace** — a full git clone on its own
branch, created for that task and thrown away after the merge. This is the one piece of
Minsky an agent has to understand to be useful in a project it manages.

## Never edit the main workspace

Start a session before changing anything:

```
minsky session start --task <taskId>
```

Every edit for that task then happens inside the session, addressed by its session id
plus a path relative to the session root. The main checkout stays clean, so two tasks
can be in flight without touching each other's files.

If you find an unrelated bug while working a task, **file a separate task for it** rather
than fixing it in this session. A session's diff should be readable as one change.

## The status lifecycle

```
TODO → PLANNING → READY → IN-PROGRESS → IN-REVIEW → DONE
```

You set the early ones deliberately. The last one you never set:

| Transition | What causes it |
| --- | --- |
| → IN-PROGRESS | starting the session |
| → IN-REVIEW | **creating the PR** — not before it |
| → DONE | **the merge, automatically** |

**Never set DONE by hand.** It is set when the PR merges, and setting it yourself skips
review, CI, and integration verification while leaving the task looking finished. The
same goes for IN-REVIEW: it means "a PR exists," so setting it while you are still
writing code makes the board lie about what is waiting on a human.

## Reaching a merge

Create the PR from the session:

```
minsky session pr create --title "<what changed>"
```

`minsky-reviewer[bot]` reviews automatically on every push — there is no command to
request a review and no human queued behind it. Read its findings, push fixes to the
same branch, and let it re-review. When it approves and checks are green, merge:

```
minsky session pr merge --task <taskId>
```

An approving review on the current head with green checks **is** the authorization to
merge; the merge itself is still an explicit call you make, not something the approval
triggers on your behalf.

## Two things that are easy to get wrong

- **Verify outcomes, not commands.** A command exiting 0 means it ran, not that it did
  what you wanted. Read the state back — re-query the setting, re-read the file, count
  the rows.
- **Commit as you go.** A session that saves everything for one final commit loses the
  history that makes a review tractable.
